WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.839
G1, the default collector, keeps eden, survivor and old, but not as three big spaces.

2
00:00:08.839 --> 00:00:11.439
It cuts the heap into equal regions.

3
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:21.364
A region is the max heap divided by 2048, kept between 1 and 32 MB, and rounded up to a power of two.

4
00:00:21.364 --> 00:00:24.255
A 1 GB heap gets 1 MB regions.

5
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:30.515
Each region has a role for a while: eden, survivor, old, humongous, or free.

6
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:33.044
New objects fill the eden regions.

7
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:41.039
A young pause copies the live objects out of every eden and survivor region, into free regions that become survivors.

8
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:46.244
The evacuated regions are free again, and next time any of them can become anything.

9
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:49.865
An object larger than half a region is humongous.

10
00:00:49.865 --> 00:00:56.869
It skips eden and gets its own run of whole regions, side by side, which count as old from the start.

11
00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:05.223
A 3 MiB array plus its 16-byte header needs four 1 MB regions, and nearly all of the fourth is wasted.

12
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:08.939
Old regions fill up slowly with promoted objects.

13
00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:17.876
When they pass a threshold, 45 percent of the heap to begin with, G1 marks the live objects in the background, while your code keeps running.

14
00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:21.363
Now it knows how much of each old region is still live.

15
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:22.966
Then come mixed pauses.

16
00:01:22.966 --> 00:01:28.139
They take all the young regions, plus a few old ones: the ones with the most garbage.

17
00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:31.485
That choice is where the name Garbage First comes from.

18
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:38.464
Copying costs what is live, so a mostly garbage region gives back the most space for the least work.

19
00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:42.475
A region more than 85 percent live is not even a candidate.

20
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:43.996
So, a check.

21
00:01:43.996 --> 00:01:51.507
Which old region does G1 take first: one that is 70 percent live, or one that is 20 percent live?

22
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.719
The 20 percent one.

23
00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:58.351
G1 copies a fifth of a region, and gets the whole region back.
